<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Our Traditional Style of Dress – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em; margin-top: 5px;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Grade:</b> 4th Grade (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Scenario:</b> Our Traditional Style of Dress</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Theme:</b> Let's Dress for a Dance.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; colspan="2""><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; colspan="3"">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: text: Interpret simple school notices.
concept: Link historical events to current events for class discussions.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can interpret simple school notices about traditional dress by explaining their meaning to peers (e.g., reads a notice that says 'Traditional Dress Day is next Friday.' and explains, 'We need to wear a pollera or traditional clothes on Friday.').
Can link the historical use of traditional clothing to current celebrations by providing simple comparisons and using visuals (e.g., show pictures of a pollera and say, 'This pollera has an old-fashioned design and this pollera has a modern design.').
Can facilitate discussions on traditional clothing and modern clothing by asking basic questions (e.g., 'Who wears this?' and points to festival images).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f0f8ff;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 34%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays various pictures of people wearing traditional Panamanian clothing (e.g., pollera, montuno) and modern clothing. Students point and say "traditional" or "modern" when the teacher asks, "Is this traditional or modern attire?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher shows a picture of a girl wearing a pollera. The teacher says, "She is wearing a beautiful pollera. It is a traditional dress. Women wear this dress at festivals and celebrations. This pollera has an old-fashioned design." The teacher models pronunciation for /w/ in 'wearing' and /t/ in 'traditional', and the blend /dr/ in 'dress'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is the pollera a modern dress or traditional clothing?" (Expected answer: Traditional!)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do we say 'She wearing' or 'She *is* wearing'?" (Expected answer: She is wearing!)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 minutes</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Our Traditional Style of Dress."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents a simple, clear school notice on a large chart or projector about the upcoming "Traditional Dress Fashion Show Presentation" (Project 2). The notice includes: Date, Time, Location, and a simple instruction like "Wear your favorite traditional attire or modern clothing!" It also mentions "showcase" and "community event."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Interpreting simple written information and understanding instructions for a school event.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher asks gist questions: "What is this notice about?" (Expected answer: A fashion show.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When is the fashion show?" (Students point to the date on the notice.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What should we wear?" (Expected answer: Traditional attire or modern clothing.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">7-10 minutes</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice interpreting notices, linking historical/modern concepts, and using Present Continuous, Present Simple, WH-questions, and target vocabulary (attire, celebration, event, community, traditional clothing, modern, old-fashioned, represent, explain, wear, during, at).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1 (Controlled Practice):</b> Students receive a worksheet with the school notice and simple True/False statements or fill-in-the-blanks about the notice. E.g., "The fashion show is on [Day of week]." "You should wear [traditional clothing/modern clothing]."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2 (Semi-controlled Substitution):</b> The teacher displays two images: one of an old-fashioned pollera and one of a modern dress. The teacher provides sentence frames: "This pollera has an ______ design." "This dress has a ______ design." Students complete the sentences with 'old-fashioned' or 'modern'. Then, in pairs, they take turns describing: "She is wearing an old-fashioned pollera. She is wearing modern attire."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3 (Pair Interaction / Peer Explanation):</b> In pairs, students receive a simplified version of the school notice. One student (A) reads the notice and explains its key points to their partner (B), mediating the information. E.g., Student A: "The notice says 'Traditional Dress Day is next Friday.' This means we need to wear a pollera or traditional clothes on Friday." Student B asks basic questions like, "What are we doing on Friday?" and "What am I wearing?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 minutes</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Our Traditional Style of Dress."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Task (Project 2: Fashion Show Presentation - Explaining Outfits):</b> Students work in small groups. Each group has pictures of 2-3 different traditional Panamanian outfits (e.g., pollera, montuno, ngäbe-buglé dress) and one modern outfit. They prepare a short presentation to explain each outfit to the class. The task requires them to mediate between the visual information and a verbal explanation, linking historical and current uses.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They use sentences like: "She is wearing a beautiful pollera." "This attire is traditional and represents our culture." "People wear this during celebrations." "What is she wearing?" (for a peer to answer). "This modern dress is different from the old-fashioned pollera."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students explain the significance: "Polleras are worn at festivals." "This jewelry is very important."</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Action:</b> The teacher circulates, offering support, prompting with WH-questions ('What is she wearing?', 'When do people wear this?'), and reminding students to use the target vocabulary and grammar.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 minutes</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during group presentations and peer assessment using a simple thumbs-up/down for clear explanations.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students present their "Fashion Show" explanations (from Stage 4). They should interpret the visual information about the clothing and explain its meaning or use to their peers, making simple comparisons between traditional and modern designs.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> Clear pronunciation of /w/ in 'wearing' and /t/ in 'traditional', and blending in 'dress' (/dr/ /ɛs/) and 'pretty' (/pr-ɪ-t-i/).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> (e.g., attire, traditional clothing, modern, old-fashioned, celebrate, represent, explain, wear, festive, beautiful, important, during, at).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity in explaining:</b> Can interpret visuals and explain their meaning to peers using present continuous and simple sentences.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Works with peers to facilitate discussions by asking basic questions and sharing information.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick exit ticket:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One thing I explained well today was..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new word I used today is..."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8-10 minutes</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What did I learn about traditional and modern clothes today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did I help my friends understand the clothes we presented?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What worked well in teaching mediation skills for traditional clothing? Students actively engaged in explaining the cultural significance and comparing clothing types. The focus on the "Fashion Show Presentation" project was motivating. What adjustments are needed for the next theme/unit? Perhaps more structured sentence frames for comparisons or specific roles for each student in group presentations to ensure equitable participation and mediation practice. Scaffold discussions more for linking historical events explicitly if students struggled with that concept.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson concludes the theme "Let's Dress for a Dance." The next lesson will introduce a new theme focusing on "My Community and Celebrations," building on the vocabulary of events and community from this unit.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">3-5 minutes</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>